# 7. Sound system — required feature

**The website must have working sound effects. Audio is not optional and must not be left as a future enhancement.**

Create a central sound manager so the effects have consistent volume and can be switched on or off.

Include the following sound events:

| Event | Required sound |
|---|---|
| Player starts the game | Aircraft/airport-inspired startup effect or boarding chime |
| Game transition | Subtle aviation transition sound |
| Correct answer | Bat hitting a cricket ball |
| Incorrect answer | Short, gentle buzzer |
| Three consecutive correct answers | Brief celebratory crowd response |
| Mystery Baggage selection | Bag-opening or reveal sound |
| Perfect 5/5 | Crowd applause or stadium cheer |
| Aircraft identification success | Subtle aviation confirmation sound |
| Boarding-pass generation | Boarding chime |
| Button interaction | Quiet interface click, where appropriate |

Use the Web Audio API for suitable synthesised effects and properly licensed local sound assets for more realistic sounds such as engines and crowd reactions.

Do not use unlicensed commercial recordings.

## Sound behaviour

- Display a clearly visible sound on/off control.
- Audio should be enabled through an initial user interaction, respecting browser autoplay restrictions.
- The player must be able to mute the game at any time.
- Remember the preference locally for future visits.
- Do not allow sounds to overlap excessively.
- Keep interface sounds short and comfortable in volume.
- The game must continue to work when audio files fail to load or audio is blocked by the browser.
- Correct and incorrect answers must still have visible feedback even when muted.

The developer must physically test the sounds in the deployed browser version. Showing a speaker icon without working playback does not fulfil this requirement.

